      "id": "word-los--body-part-owner-our",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "los",
      "answerEn": "our here; Spanish uses los before a body part whose owner is already clear",
      "noteEn": "Spanish often uses el, la, los, or las before a body part when the surrounding verb or context already identifies its owner. Natural English often supplies ‘my,’ ‘your,’ ‘our,’ or another possessive word instead.",
      "example": { "es": "Cruzamos los brazos.", "en": "We cross our arms." },
      "tags": ["word", "definite article", "body part", "possession", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-entran--fit-opening",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "entran",
      "answerEn": "they fit; they go in",
      "noteEn": "Entrar often means ‘to enter’ or ‘to go in.’ With an object and an opening or available space, it can say that the object fits into that space.",
      "example": { "es": "Las cajas entran en el armario.", "en": "The boxes fit in the cupboard." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "fit", "A2"]
    },
